n{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/state.json" "file_sha256" "eca123e1f23257b5e527d038cb945846f6f4d5b321191a191755ec365d6cf2e3"} {"question" "q-41944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/state.json" "file_sha256" "eca123e1f23257b5e527d038cb945846f6f4d5b321191a191755ec365d6cf2e3"} {"question" "q-41944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4365440057493f60dc29fe8f63f7736b97744b7a6b03774345cdab7e0f4a7dd7"} {"question" "q-41944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/state.json" "file_sha256" "eca123e1f23257b5e527d038cb945846f6f4d5b321191a191755ec365d6cf2e3"} {"question" "q-41944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/state.json" "file_sha256" "eca123e1f23257b5e527d038cb945846f6f4d5b321191a191755ec365d6cf2e3"} {"question" "q-41944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/state.json" "file_sha256" "eca123e1f23257b5e527d038cb945846f6f4d5b321191a191755ec365d6cf2e3"} {"question" "e-4408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/example-4408.json" "file_sha256" "00991ba6f54b2c41bbd5bd22a2cf649259caae4d55dc3532a32705de0a65d816"} {"question" "e-4408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/example-4408.json" "file_sha256" "00991ba6f54b2c41bbd5bd22a2cf649259caae4d55dc3532a32705de0a65d816"} {"question" "e-4410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/example-4410.json" "file_sha256" "389140b0a38fbc4fd076fc95bb794fe1f90434f228c66aedd9dd381018684d16"} {"question" "e-4410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978659/example-4410.json" "file_sha256" "389140b0a38fbc4fd076fc95bb794fe1f90434f228c66aedd9dd381018684d16"}] "usage" {"e-4408" [] "q-41949" [] "q-13762" [] "e-4410" []} "basis" 839 "retractions" [[17592186101054 :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_{2}B$ given that $I_{2}$ is the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix and\n\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186101315 :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186100609 :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186100609 :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too.\n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186100610 :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186100610 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3$.\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3$, the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 0 \\\\ -1 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 1 + (-4) \\cdot 0 \\\\ 5 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098095 17592186098096 17592186098097 17592186098098 17592186098099 17592186099487 17592186099488 17592186099489 17592186099490 17592186099491] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098094 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098094 :answer-field/id] [17592186098094 :answer-field/key] [17592186098094 :answer-field/type] [17592186099486 :answer-field/correct] [17592186099486 :answer-field/id] [17592186099486 :answer-field/key] [17592186099486 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
